{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This affects Layer 5 Workroom authority/projection facts and Layer 7 UI signing surfaces. Kernel validity, signatures, and sequencing are unchanged. The correct dependency direction is preserved: the browser may give early refusal from current projected facts, while the fold remains authoritative. I agree with the implementation's satisfier dispatch: originating-requester ratification requires participation, role-qualified ratification requires the role and not separate participation, and own-author supersede remains available. But the intended Layer-7 contract changes from render-time affordances to a final current-state check immediately before durable signing. The exact head leaves docs/reference/architecture.md byte-identical to 97a and republishes it unchanged, despite child 703ca542 requiring the update. Document the affordance/final-signing distinction and the exact state, ratify, and own-author supersede rules, including effective and unretired target standing.\n\nSECURITY. Changes are required. The fold rejects ratification when the target decision is not effective and when the target is retired before testing satisfier authority. App.doAct resolves the current statement, but signingRefusal checks only target existence then mayRatify; it receives no current decision and ignores target.retired. I reproduced the exact head returning ALLOWED for both retired and ineffective targets. A control offered while standing can therefore still call api.act after the target loses standing, appending a permanently ineffective row. This is integrity/log pollution, not privilege escalation, because the server fold still gives it no force. Pass the current target decision and retirement into a fail-closed ratify guard before mayRatify. Add direct retired/ineffective cases plus rendered App and Thread mutation regressions that change lease, membership, or target standing after the control is offered and assert zero api.act calls. The current signing-boundary tests call only the pure helper, so bypassing either wiring site stays green.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The single dispatcher reusing isLiveParticipant and mayRatify is the smallest clear shape; keep it. Narrow or document the blanket supersede exemption as the current own-author withdraw route rather than a general supersede promise. Also correct the claim that every durable browser act passes through signingRefusal: artifact publication uses publishRefusal separately. The comment-only cache correction is valid: internal/app/app_test.go changes only stale prose, not constants or assertions.\n\nEVIDENCE. Planner's three claimed mutations reproduce: forcing all ratification through participation fails the role-only and originating-requester cases; removing supersede exemption fails departed own-author cleanup; dropping state membership fails departed state write. Focused unmodified tests pass, npm tests pass 105/105, internal/app tests pass, gofmt/build/vet/diff-check pass, make ui-check byte-reproduces the embed, exact head equals origin/request/planner-dr-recut, and the worktree is clean. Those green gates do not cover the blockers. Do not merge this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:52e3acfdab5c075526e00992e9e3d1975846b570","head":"6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cef5b12c9c2a1f66ee98c4e3631d6c7db2c2152f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cf9080db2580db92e8d58976acd89979657c9b00","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}